course: dictionary
lesson: 15317
title: Smokeless
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Smokeless is made of two parts: smoke and -less. The ending -less means without, so smokeless means without smoke. «The camp stove gave a smokeless flame, so the air stayed clear.» The clue is clear air: no smoke was there to spoil it.
explain: Fuel can be smokeless too. «Smokeless fuel gives out heat with very little smoke when it burns.» Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence. If smokeless means without smoke, the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Smokeless is also a law word in the UK. «The street is a smokeless zone, so only fuels that make little smoke may be burned.» A smokeless zone is an area with rules about what people may burn. Read the clues around the word to see what it means.
fact: Smokeless means giving off little or no smoke, or being without smoke.
fact: Smokeless is made from smoke and the ending -less, which means without.
fact: «Smokeless» fuel burns with little or no smoke, so it is better for the air.
fact: After the deadly London smog of 1952, Britain passed the Clean Air Act and made smokeless zones.
fact: In a smokeless zone, only fuels that make little smoke may be burned.
exercise: «The stove gave a smokeless flame, so the kitchen stayed clear.» What does smokeless mean? | Full of smoke | Very hot | Made of glass | *Without smoke
exercise: The word smokeless is made of smoke and which ending? | -ing | -ful | -ly | *-less
exercise: Britain made smokeless zones after which event? | *The deadly London smog of 1952 | The invention of the bicycle | A long dry summer in the countryside | The first football World Cup
test: «We sat under a smokeless sky after the storm.» What does smokeless mean here? | Full of smoke from the fires | Dark with thick fog | Cloudy and grey | *Clear, with no smoke
test: In a smokeless zone, which fuels may be burned? | Only wood that is wet | Fuels that make thick, black smoke | Any fuel at all | *Only fuels that make little smoke
exam: Which best explains smokeless fuel? | Fuel that cannot burn at all | Fuel that is always wet | Fuel that makes lots of thick smoke | *Fuel that burns with little or no smoke
